=== GAP-054 === {'gap_id': 'GAP-054', 'category': 'SW', 'priority': 'P3', 'status': 'CLOSED-DUPLICATE', 'description': "A DOI-LESS SOURCE HAS NO SANCTIONED WAY TO CORRECT A BIBLIOGRAPHIC FIELD AFTER ADMISSION. Found admitting REF-01010 (the Uganda Building Control Code 2019, a statutory instrument, no DOI): add-source's own --pages flag was simply not passed at admission (my own omission, not a tooling defect on its own), but once the row exists, nothing can fix it -- amend-source explicitly REFUSES bibliographic fields ('use db.py correct-source, which takes it from the logged payload'), and correct-source itself REFUSES outright for any ref_id with no DOI ('no DOI, so no payload can be keyed to it'). This closes the loop with no writer: research_batch_dod's R3 (clause/section/page citation on regulatory-stratum sources) reads evidence_sources.pages/article_number specifically -- not source_value_extractions' own loc_section/loc_subsection/loc_clause, which REF-01010's extraction_id=70 DOES carry correctly (loc_section=12, loc_subsection=3, loc_clause=a) -- so a source can be genuinely, precisely clause-cited at the extraction level and still fail R3 forever, for every DOI-less regulatory/statutory/code source this project ever admits, with no remediation path except getting --pages right on the FIRST add-source call. WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT: either (a) correct-source gains a DOI-less path keyed to a persisted retrieval-log artefact instead of a DOI'd payload (the artefact IS the authority for a statute the way a CrossRef record is for a journal article), or (b) R3's own check reads source_value_extractions.loc_* as an alternative satisfying condition to evidence_sources.pages/article_number, since the extraction-level locator is where R3's actual concern (a clause a reader can find) is already precisely recorded. This batch's own occurrence is recorded as a REASONED WAIVER, not remediated: REF-01010.pages stays unset; the true locator is source_value_extractions.extraction_id=70 (loc_section=12, loc_subsection=3, loc_clause=a), byte-verified against the persisted primary PDF (retrieval-log/session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection/35c03adeb327d61c.pdf, page 15 of 42). || CORRECTED 2026-09-28: CLOSED AS A DUPLICATE, caught by an independent adversarial pass, not self-caught: this is GAP-043 item (1) refiled under a new id -- same mechanism ('correct-source keys its payload BY DOI... amend-source refuses anything in _CORRECTABLE by design. So for a row with no DOI both columns are now unreachable by EVERY sanctioned writer'), same conclusion, filed twice. Rule 5 ('never write the same fact into a second table') applies to the gaps register too. The genuinely NEW content here (REF-01010 as a concrete pages/article_number instance rather than journal_name/publisher; the observation that R3's check reads evidence_sources.pages/article_number and not source_value_extractions.loc_*, so a precisely clause-cited extraction can still fail R3 forever) is folded into GAP-043 instead. See GAP-043 for the live record; this id is superseded by it.", 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection'}

=== GAP-055 === {'gap_id': 'GAP-055', 'category': 'SW', 'priority': 'P2', 'status': 'OPEN', 'description': "close-adversarial-pass CANNOT BE SATISFIED BY A GOOD-FAITH, WELL-FORMED SURVIVED FINDING, AND NOTHING CAN FIX ONE AFTER RECORDING. Found closing batch 21's adversarial pass (pass_id=2): close_adversarial_pass() requires every SURVIVED-verdict finding's artefact field to resolve, via dbcore.resolve_under, to exactly one existing file under the repo (a bare path, no delimiters, no row/line references). All 9 of this pass's SURVIVED findings fail this: the antagonist wrote richer, information-dense citations for several of them -- two files joined with '; ' or 'and' (e.g. 'retrieval-log/.../18ef7d575e63330c.xml; 6ab420aabe807589.zip'), a code symbol plus file ('schemas/tier_derivation.py TIER_MAP'), a table-and-row reference ('source_value_extractions rows 70 and 71 (batch21.db)'), a DB-column-and-file pairing ('evidence_sources.co1_provenance REF-01009 vs 18ef7d575e63330c.xml') -- none of which is a bare resolvable path, though every one names something real and checkable. THE ANTAGONIST'S OWN INSTRUCTIONS (.claude/agents/antagonist.md, 'Report shape') DO show the correct bare-path format in their worked example, so this is a genuine deviation by the reviewing agent, not solely a gate-design problem -- but the consequence is disproportionate: once record-adversarial-pass has parsed the findings block from the (tracked, immutable) reviewer transcript, NOTHING can correct a SURVIVED row's artefact field. dispose-adversarial-finding only writes disposition/disposition_ref, and only for a finding at all (its own CLI does not gate by verdict, but nothing in db.py updates artefact for any verdict). record-adversarial-pass itself refuses a second pass for the same subject_session ('at most one adversarial pass per research batch'). So a single formatting slip by the reviewing model, on a SURVIVED (not even a SUSTAINED) finding, permanently blocks close-adversarial-pass and therefore adversarial_pass_recorded/research_batch_dod for the whole batch, with no remediation path short of a schema-level fix. THIS BATCH'S OWN INSTANCE: pass_id=2 is RECORDED but left OPEN, as a reasoned waiver (research_batch_dod has no R-rule for this specifically, but adversarial_pass_audit.py --session reports 'FAIL: 1 pass(es) recorded... none is CLOSED'). WHAT WOULD CLOSE THIS GAP: (a) a writer that can correct a SURVIVED finding's artefact field, symmetrical to dispose-adversarial-finding for SUSTAINED ones (with its own --reason, so a correction is not silent); or (b) close_adversarial_pass accepting a semicolon/'and'-delimited list of paths, checking that at least one resolves, rather than requiring the whole string to resolve as one path; or (c) the antagonist agent's own report-writing being hardened (a self-check before hand-back: does every SURVIVED artefact field parse as a single existing path) so this is caught before the transcript is finalised and immutable.", 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection'}

